[?25l[0m[38;5;141m> [0m[38;5;252m[1m## What was wrong and what was changed[0m[0m
[0m[0m
This is an [Underhanded C Contest](http://www.underhanded-c.org/) entry. The C code looks like a simple luggage routing system, but it has a deliberate trick: [1mcomments longer than 80 characters overflow and get re-parsed as new routing directives[22m, allowing an attacker to inject phantom luggage routing records by embedding them inside comment text.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The original Rust translation failed to replicate three critical aspects of C's [38;5;10mscanf[0m behavior:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;252m[1m### Bug 1: Unsigned-only integer parsing[0m[0m
The Rust [38;5;10mscan_uint[0m function only parsed unsigned digits ([38;5;10m0-9[0m). The C code uses [38;5;10mscanf("%d")[0m, which reads [1msigned[22m integers. When a comment overflows and the leftover starts with something like [38;5;10m-12[0m, C reads it as the signed integer -12, which when stored in an [38;5;10munsigned int[0m wraps to [38;5;10m4294967284[0m. The Rust code saw [38;5;10m-[0m as a non-digit and broke out of the loop entirely.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mFix[22m: Replaced [38;5;10mscan_uint[0m with [38;5;10mscan_int_with_ws[0m that handles optional [38;5;10m-[0m/[38;5;10m+[0m signs and casts through [38;5;10mi32[0m → [38;5;10mu32[0m to replicate C's signed-to-unsigned wrapping.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;252m[1m### Bug 2: Breaking on scanf partial match failure[0m[0m
In C, when [38;5;10mscanf("%8[A-Z0-9]")[0m encounters a character outside the scanset (e.g., lowercase [38;5;10mt[0m in [38;5;10mtoo cold[0m), it returns [38;5;10m0[0m (zero items matched), [1mnot[22m [38;5;10mEOF[0m. The C code only checks [38;5;10m== EOF[0m, so it [1mdoesn't break[22m — it continues to the next [38;5;10mscanf[0m call. The local variables that weren't written to retain their values from the previous loop iteration (C stack reuse).[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The Rust code used [38;5;10mmatch ... None => break[0m for every field, which is equivalent to breaking on [38;5;10mscanf[0m returning 0 — wrong behavior.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mFix[22m: When a scanset match fails, instead of breaking, the code now retains the previous iteration's value for that field (using [38;5;10mprev_*[0m variables that persist across loop iterations).[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;252m[1m### Bug 3: Whitespace consumption on [38;5;10m%d[0m failure[0m[0m
C's [38;5;10mscanf("%d")[0m skips leading whitespace as part of the [38;5;10m%d[0m conversion specifier, [1meven when the conversion itself fails[22m. So given input [38;5;10m UA112358...[0m, [38;5;10mscanf("%d")[0m consumes the space, fails on [38;5;10mU[0m, and leaves the position at [38;5;10mU[0m. The subsequent [38;5;10mscanf("%8[A-Z0-9]")[0m then successfully reads [38;5;10mUA112358[0m.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The original Rust code didn't advance the input pointer past whitespace when integer parsing failed, so the next scanset saw the space character and also failed.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mFix[22m: [38;5;10mscan_int_with_ws[0m always returns the post-whitespace position, even on matching failure, so subsequent scanset parsers see the correct input position.[0m[0m
